{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Microcachrys tetragona (Creeping Pine or Creeping Strawberry Pine) is a species of dioecious conifer belonging to the podocarp family (Podocarpaceae).","title":"Microcachrys"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the sole species of the genus Microcachrys.","title":"Microcachrys"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The plant is endemic to western Tasmania, where it is a low shrub growing to 1 m tall at high altitudes.","title":"Microcachrys"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its leaves are scale-like, arranged (unusually for the Podocarpaceae) in opposite decussate pairs, superficially resembling those of the unrelated "Diselma archeri" (Cupressaceae).","title":"Microcachrys"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" It shares the common name Creeping pine with several other plants.","title":"Microcachrys"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Females produce tiny, red, edible berries in summer.","title":"Microcachrys"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Centro Poliesportivo Pinheiro, also known as the Pinheirão is a football stadium inaugurated on June 15, 1985 in Curitiba, Paraná, Brazil, with a maximum capacity of 35,000 people.","title":"Pinheirão"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The stadium is owned by the Paranaense Football Federation (meaning "Federação Paranaense de Futebol" in Portuguese), and was the home ground of Paraná Clube.","title":"Pinheirão"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Its formal name means Pinheiro Multi-Modality Center and honors the Pine tree, which is a common tree in the state.","title":"Pinheirão"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Cajeput tree is a common name used for certain classification of tree that has a white spongy bark that is flexible and can easily flake off the trunk.","title":"Cajeput tree"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Cajeput tree is of the genus "Melaleuca", native to Australia and is commonly known in North America as the Tea Tree.","title":"Cajeput tree"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The name "Cajeput tree" is primarily used for the species "M. cajuputi", "M. leucadendra", "M. linariifolia", "M. viridiflora" and "M. quinquenervia".","title":"Cajeput tree"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Other names for these trees are the paperbark tree, punk tree, or the white bottle brush tree.","title":"Cajeput tree"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Similar subtropical trees from the eucalyptus family are evergreens with pointed leaves and white, red or green flowers.","title":"Cajeput tree"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Aborigines of Australia often used the cajeput tree bark for shields, canoes, roofing material and timber.","title":"Cajeput tree"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Tetradium is a genus of nine species of trees in the family Rutaceae, occurring in temperate to tropical east Asia.","title":"Tetradium"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In older books, the genus was often included in the related genus "Euodia" (sometimes written, ""Evodia"" from latin spelling), but that genus is now restricted to tropical species.","title":"Tetradium"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In cultivation in English-speaking countries, they are known as Euodia, Evodia, or Bee bee tree.","title":"Tetradium"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Banksia sessilis, commonly known as parrot bush, is a species of shrub or tree in the plant genus "Banksia" in the family Proteaceae.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It had been known as Dryandra sessilis until 2007, when the genus "Dryandra" was sunk into "Banksia".","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Noongar peoples know the plant as Budjan or Butyak.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Widespread throughout southwest Western Australia, it is found on sandy soils over laterite or limestone, often as an understorey plant in open forest, woodland or shrubland.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Encountered as a shrub or small tree up to 6 m in height, it has prickly dark green leaves and dome-shaped cream-yellow flowerheads.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Flowering from winter through to late spring, it provides a key source of food—both the nectar and the insects it attracts—for honeyeaters in the cooler months, and species diversity is reduced in areas where there is little or no parrot bush occurring.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Several species of honeyeater, some species of native bee, and the European honey bee seek out and consume the nectar, while the long-billed black cockatoo and Australian ringneck eat the seed.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The life cycle of "Banksia sessilis" is adapted to regular bushfires.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Killed by fire and regenerating by seed afterwards, each shrub generally produces many flowerheads and a massive amount of seed.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" It can recolonise disturbed areas, and may grow in thickets.","title":"Banksia sessilis"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"A bee tree is a tree in which a colony of honey bees makes its home.","title":"Bee tree"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A colony of bees may live in a bee tree for many years.","title":"Bee tree"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Most bee trees have a large inner hollow, often with an upper and lower entrance.","title":"Bee tree"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Microcachrys
